            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus understands 'light' as a polyvalent symbol primarily representing God's presence, Christ, truth, and salvation, while also widely recognizing it as symbolizing God's law, moral purity, and the visible good works of believers.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines 'light' exclusively as the visible practice of the law and good works that others can see.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Divine presence, truth, and life, which extends derivatively to the law and believers' righteous conduct.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Visible good works and the lived law.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book restricts the symbol to its moral and behavioral application (human good works and the law), omitting the primary consensus referents of God, Christ, and salvation.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no direct contradiction, as the book's definition perfectly matches a well-established secondary meaning within the consensus (specifically for the verses it cites). The disagreement lies only in the book's exclusion of the broader, primary theological referents.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The consensus explicitly includes both the law (Prov 6:23) and the visible good works of believers (Matt 5:16) as standard, common meanings for 'light.' The book adopts these exact derivative meanings but narrows the overall scope of the symbol by leaving out the primary Christological and theological dimensions.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"In biblical theology, light overwhelmingly and fundamentally points to God (1 John 1:5) and Christ (John 8:12); defining it solely as human works or the law risks mistaking the reflection for the source.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
